1	# MCP Memory Server
2	
3	This server implements long-term memory capabilities for AI assistants using mem0 principles, powered by PostgreSQL with pgvector for efficient vector similarity search.
4	
5	## Features
6	
7	- PostgreSQL with pgvector for vector similarity search
8	- Automatic embedding generation using BERT
9	- RESTful API for memory operations
10	- Semantic search capabilities
11	- Support for different types of memories (learnings, experiences, etc.)
12	- Tag-based memory retrieval
13	- Confidence scoring for memories
14	- Server-Sent Events (SSE) for real-time updates
15	- Cursor MCP protocol compatible
16	
17	## Prerequisites
18	
19	1. PostgreSQL 14+ with pgvector extension installed:
20	```bash
21	# In your PostgreSQL instance:
22	CREATE EXTENSION vector;
23	```
24	
25	2. Node.js 16+
26	
27	## Setup
28	
29	1. Install dependencies:
30	```bash
31	npm install
32	```
33	
34	2. Configure environment variables:
35	Copy `.env.sample` to `.env` and adjust the values:
36	```bash
37	cp .env.sample .env
38	```
39	
40	Example `.env` configurations:
41	```bash
42	# With username/password
43	DATABASE_URL="postgresql://username:password@localhost:5432/mcp_memory"
44	PORT=3333
45	
46	# Local development with peer authentication
47	DATABASE_URL="postgresql:///mcp_memory"
48	PORT=3333
49	```
50	
51	3. Initialize the database:
52	```bash
53	npm run prisma:migrate
54	```
55	
56	4. Start the server:
57	```bash
58	npm start
59	```
60	
61	For development with auto-reload:
62	```bash
63	npm run dev
64	```
65	
66	## Using with Cursor
67	
68	### Adding the MCP Server in Cursor
69	
70	To add the memory server to Cursor, you need to modify your MCP configuration file located at `~/.cursor/mcp.json`. Add the following configuration to the `mcpServers` object:
71	
72	```json
73	{
74	  "mcpServers": {
75	    "memory": {
76	      "command": "node",
77	      "args": [
78	        "/path/to/your/memory/src/server.js"
79	      ]
80	    }
81	  }
82	}
83	```
84	
85	Replace `/path/to/your/memory` with the actual path to your memory server installation.
86	
87	For example, if you cloned the repository to `/Users/username/workspace/memory`, your configuration would look like:
88	
89	```json
90	{
91	  "mcpServers": {
92	    "memory": {
93	      "command": "node",
94	      "args": [
95	        "/Users/username/workspace/memory/src/server.js"
96	      ]
97	    }
98	  }
99	}
100	```
101	
102	The server will be automatically started by Cursor when needed. You can verify it's working by:
103	1. Opening Cursor
104	2. The memory server will be started automatically when Cursor launches
105	3. You can check the server status by visiting `http://localhost:3333/mcp/v1/health`
106	
107	### Available MCP Endpoints
108	
109	#### SSE Connection
110	- **Endpoint**: `GET /mcp/v1/sse`
111	- **Query Parameters**:
112	  - `subscribe`: Comma-separated list of events to subscribe to (optional)
113	- **Events**:
114	  - `connected`: Sent on initial connection
115	  - `memory.created`: Sent when new memories are created
116	  - `memory.updated`: Sent when existing memories are updated
117	
118	#### Memory Operations
119	1. **Create Memory**
120	```http
121	POST /mcp/v1/memory
122	Content-Type: application/json
123	
124	{
125	  "type": "learning",
126	  "content": {
127	    "topic": "Express.js",
128	    "details": "Express.js is a web application framework for Node.js"
129	  },
130	  "source": "documentation",
131	  "tags": ["nodejs", "web-framework"],
132	  "confidence": 0.95
133	}
134	```
135	
136	2. **Search Memories**
137	```http
138	GET /mcp/v1/memory/search?query=web+frameworks&type=learning&tags=nodejs
139	```
140	
141	3. **List Memories**
142	```http
143	GET /mcp/v1/memory?type=learning&tags=nodejs,web-framework
144	```
145	
146	### Health Check
147	```http
148	GET /mcp/v1/health
149	```
150	
151	### Response Format
152	All API responses follow the standard MCP format:
153	```json
154	{
155	  "status": "success",
156	  "data": {
157	    // Response data
158	  }
159	}
160	```
161	
162	Or for errors:
163	```json
164	{
165	  "status": "error",
166	  "error": "Error message"
167	}
168	```
169	
170	## Memory Schema
171	
172	- id: Unique identifier
173	- type: Type of memory (learning, experience, etc.)
174	- content: Actual memory content (JSON)
175	- source: Where the memory came from
176	- embedding: Vector representation of the content (384 dimensions)
177	- tags: Array of relevant tags
178	- confidence: Confidence score (0-1)
179	- createdAt: When the memory was created
180	- updatedAt: When the memory was last updated 

